meta {
  name: Replace Carrier Appointments
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

put {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/agency/carrier-appointments
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{appointmentsOwnerToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

script:pre-request {
  // A fresh code every run. `(carrierId, codeKey)` is unique platform-wide and
  // a repeat is answered with 409 by design, so a hard-coded value would pass
  // once and then fail on every subsequent `bru run` against the same database.
  bru.setVar("appointmentCode", `BRU${Date.now().toString().slice(-6)}`);
}

body:json {
  {
    "appointments": [
      {
        "carrierId": "{{appointmentCarrierId}}",
        "carrierAgencyCode": "{{appointmentCode}}",
        "isPrimary": true,
        "active": true
      }
    ]
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("stores the code as issued and makes it primary", function () {
    const appointment = res.getBody().appointments[0];
    expect(appointment.carrierAgencyCode).to.equal(bru.getVar("appointmentCode"));
    expect(appointment.carrierName).to.equal("Allstate");
    expect(appointment.isPrimary).to.equal(true);
  });
}

docs {
  # PUT /agency/carrier-appointments — replace the whole list

  - **Module:** none (`@SkipModule()`)
  - **Permission:** `agency:carrier_appointments:write`
  - **Data scope:** none (`@SkipBranch()`)

  ## Why `PUT` and why the whole list

  The semantics genuinely are replacement — `PATCH /platform/agencies/:id/modules`
  merges, this does not. And the list is the unit because two of its rules are
  properties of the *list*: exactly one primary, and no duplicate
  `(carrier, code)` pairs. Three granular endpoints would mean three copies of
  each. An appointment also has no id — the sub-document is `_id: false` — so
  there is nothing to address a granular route at.

  ⚠ Two people editing at once will clobber each other. Accepted, not
  overlooked: there is no `If-Match` convention anywhere in this API, and
  inventing one here would make this the only endpoint with one.

  ## Body
  ```json
  { "appointments": [
      { "carrierId": "6a86…", "carrierAgencyCode": "A0B9049",
        "isPrimary": true, "active": true } ] }
  ```

  - `carrierAgencyCode` is **optional**. A row without one is dropped, not
    stored — that is how the owner leaves a carrier for later, and it is what
    keeps the code required on a stored appointment (a code-less element would
    index as `(carrier, null)` and collide with every other such agency).
  - `carrierId` must be a **global** carrier. An agency-scoped one is a `400`.
  - `isPrimary` and `active` both default sensibly: the first row becomes
    primary, and a primary flagged onto an inactive row moves to the first
    active one.
  - The code is stored **as issued**; matching uses a trimmed, upper-cased key
    derived by `appointmentCodeKey`, which the mailer pipeline also calls on a
    file's `agencyid`.

  ## Errors
  - `400` — a carrier that is not global; a `carrierId` that is not an
    ObjectId; more than one primary; the same pair listed twice.
  - `403 Forbidden` — missing `agency:carrier_appointments:write`.
  - `409 Conflict` — another agency already holds that `(carrier, code)`. The
    message names the holder. ⚠ Deactivating an appointment does **not** free
    its code; only removing it does.
}
